{:edb/db-before-t 1466 :edb/db-after-t 1467 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534779 50 #inst "2026-10-08T06:39:46.201Z" 13194139534779 true] [17592186133505 1100 "Let $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ be vectors such that $\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert = 6$ and $\\Vert \\mathbf{u}+4\\mathbf{v} \\Vert = 25$. If $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$, find $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{u}$." 13194139534779 false] [17592186133505 1100 "Let $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ be vectors such that $\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert = 6$ and $\\Vert \\mathbf{u}+4\\mathbf{v} \\Vert = 25.$  If $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v},$ find $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{u}.$" 13194139534779 true] [17592186133505 1145 "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$, we also have that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $4\\mathbf{v}$. So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖u + 4v‖^{2} &= ‖u‖^{2} + ‖4v‖^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖u + 4v‖^{2} &= ‖u‖^{2} + (4‖v‖)^{2} \\\\\n‖u + 4v‖^{2} &= ‖u‖^{2} + 16‖v‖^{2} \\\\\n(25)^{2} &= ‖u‖^{2} + 16(6)^{2} \\\\\n625 &= ‖u‖^{2} + 576 \\\\\n‖u‖^{2} &= 625 - 576 \\\\\n‖u‖^{2} &= 49\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{u}=\\Vert\\mathbf{u}\\Vert^2=49\n$$" 13194139534779 false] [17592186133505 1145 "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v},$ we also have that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $4\\mathbf{v}.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+4v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+‖4v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+4v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+(4‖v‖)^{2} \\\\ ‖u+4v{‖}^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+16‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ (25)^{2} & =‖u{‖}^{2}+16(6)^{2} \\\\ 625 & =‖u{‖}^{2}+576 \\\\ ‖u{‖}^{2} & =625-576 \\\\ ‖u{‖}^{2} & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{u}=\\Vert\\mathbf{u}\\Vert^2=49.$" 13194139534779 true] [17592186133506 1100 "Given that $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} +3\\mathbf{v} \\Vert=14$ and $\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert=2$, find the smallest possible value of $\\Vert \\mathbf{u}\\Vert$." 13194139534779 false] [17592186133506 1100 "Given that $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} +3\\mathbf{v} \\Vert=14$ and $\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert=2,$ find the smallest possible value of $\\Vert \\mathbf{u}\\Vert.$" 13194139534779 true] [17592186133506 1145 "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+3v‖ & \\le ‖u‖+‖3v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖u+3v‖ & \\le ‖u‖+3‖v‖ \\\\ 14 & \\le ‖u‖+3(2) \\\\ 14 & \\le ‖u‖+6 \\\\ 8 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert$ is $8.$" 13194139534779 true] [17592186133506 1145 "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n‖u + 3v‖ \\mid \\le ‖u‖ + ‖3v‖\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}‖u + 3v‖ & \\le ‖u‖ + 3‖v‖ \\\\ 14 & \\le ‖u‖ + 3(2) \\\\ 14 & \\le ‖u‖ + 6 \\\\ 8 & \\le ‖u‖\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $\\Vert \\mathbf{u} \\Vert$ is $8$." 13194139534779 false] [17592186133507 1100 "Let the vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ be orthogonal.\nIf $\\mid \\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\mid ^2+5\\Vert\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w}\\Vert^2=29$ and $\\| \\mathbf{u} \\| = \\| \\mathbf{w} \\| = 2$, then what is the minimum possible value of $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert$?" 13194139534779 false] [17592186133507 1100 "Let the vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ be orthogonal. \nIf $|\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}|^2+5\\Vert\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w}\\Vert^2=29$ and $\\| \\mathbf{u} \\|  = \\| \\mathbf{w} \\| = 2$, then what is the minimum possible value of $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert?$" 13194139534779 true] [17592186133507 1145 "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n29 &= \\mid u \\cdot v \\mid ^{2} + 5‖v + w‖^{2} \\\\\n&\\le ‖u‖^{2} \\cdot ‖v‖^{2} + 5‖v + w‖^{2} \\\\\n&= 2^{2} \\cdot ‖v‖^{2} + 5‖v + w‖^{2} \\\\\n&= 4‖v‖^{2} + 5‖v + w‖^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{w}$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4‖v‖^{2} + 5‖v + w‖^{2} &= 4‖v‖^{2} + 5(‖v‖^{2} + ‖w‖^{2}) \\\\\n&\\le 4‖v‖^{2} + 5(‖v‖^{2} + 2^{2}) \\\\\n&= 9‖v‖^{2} + 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}29 & \\le 9‖v‖^{2} + 20 \\\\ 9 & \\le 9‖v‖^{2} \\\\ 1 & \\le ‖v‖^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n1 \\leq \\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert^2\n$$\n\nimplies that\n\n$$\n1 \\leq \\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert\n$$\n\n. Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert$ is $1$." 13194139534779 false] [17592186133507 1145 "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}29 & =|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {‖u{‖}^{2}\\cdot ‖v‖}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot ‖v{‖}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =4‖v{‖}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{w}$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4‖v{‖}^{2}+5‖v+w{‖}^{2} & =4‖v{‖}^{2}+5(‖v{‖}^{2}+‖w{‖}^{2}) \\\\  & \\le 4‖v{‖}^{2}+5(‖v{‖}^{2}+{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =9‖v{‖}^{2}+20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}29 & \\le 9‖v{‖}^{2}+20 \\\\ 9 & \\le 9‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 1 & \\le ‖v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $1 \\leq \\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert^2$ implies that $1 \\leq \\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert$. Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $\\Vert\\mathbf{v}\\Vert$ is $1.$" 13194139534779 true] [17592186133698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309739 13194139534779 true] [17592186133698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309746 13194139534779 true] [17592186133698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309760 13194139534779 true] [17592186133698 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309767 13194139534779 true] [17592186133699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309704 13194139534779 true] [17592186133699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309711 13194139534779 true] [17592186133699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309753 13194139534779 true] [17592186133699 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309774 13194139534779 true] [17592186133700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309718 13194139534779 true] [17592186133700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309725 13194139534779 true] [17592186133700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309732 13194139534779 true] [17592186133700 1020 #edb/ref 17592186309781 13194139534779 true] [17592186134141 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534779 true] [17592186134141 1100 "Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors such that $‖v‖ = 7$ and $‖2u + v‖ = 25$. If $u$ is orthogonal to $v$, find $‖u‖$." 13194139534779 false] [17592186134141 1100 "Let $u$ and $v$ be vectors such that $‖v‖=7$ and $‖2u+v‖=25.$  If $u$ is orthogonal to $v$, find $‖u‖.$" 13194139534779 true] [17592186134141 1145 "Since $u$ is orthogonal to $v,$ we also have that $2u$ is orthogonal to $v.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖2u+v{‖}^{2} & =‖2u{‖}^{2}+‖v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖2u+v{‖}^{2} & =(2‖u‖)^{2}+‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ ‖2u+v{‖}^{2} & =4‖u{‖}^{2}+‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ (25)^{2} & =4‖u{‖}^{2}+(7)^{2} \\\\ 625 & =4‖u{‖}^{2}+49 \\\\ 576 & =4‖u{‖}^{2} \\\\ ‖u{‖}^{2} & =144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $‖u‖=12.$" 13194139534779 true] [17592186134150 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534779 true] [17592186134150 1100 "Let the vectors $v$ and $w$ be orthogonal.\n\nIf $\\mid u \\cdot v \\mid ^{2} + ‖v + w‖^{2} = 50$ and $‖u‖ = ‖w‖ = 4$, then what is the minimum possible value of $‖v‖$?" 13194139534779 false] [17592186134150 1100 "Let the vectors $v$ and $w$ be orthogonal. \nIf $|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+‖v+w{‖}^{2}=50$ and $‖u‖=‖w‖=4$, then what is the minimum possible value of $‖v‖?$" 13194139534779 true] [17592186134150 1145 "First, using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =|u\\cdot v{|}^{2}+‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & \\le {‖u{‖}^{2}\\cdot ‖v‖}^{2}+‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2}\\cdot ‖v{‖}^{2}+‖v+w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =16‖v{‖}^{2}+‖v+w{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v⊥w$, using the Pythagorean theorem, simplifying, and substituting in the given information, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16‖v{‖}^{2}+‖v+w{‖}^{2} & =16‖v{‖}^{2}+‖v{‖}^{2}+‖w{‖}^{2} \\\\  & =16‖v{‖}^{2}+‖v{‖}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =17‖v{‖}^{2}+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining the two steps above, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & \\le 17‖v‖+16 \\\\ 34 & \\le 17‖v{‖}^{2} \\\\ 2 & \\le ‖v{‖}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $2\\le ‖v{‖}^{2}$ implies that $\\sqrt{2}\\le ‖v‖.$ Notice that we disregard the negative solutions since the norm can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of $‖v‖$ is $\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139534779 true] [17592186134200 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534779 true] [17592186134200 1100 "Given that $‖7u + v‖ = 32$ and $‖v‖ = 4$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$." 13194139534779 false] [17592186134200 1100 "Given that $‖7u+v‖=32$ and $‖v‖=4$, find the smallest possible value of $‖u‖.$" 13194139534779 true] [17592186134200 1145 "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖7u+v‖ & \\le ‖7u‖+‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖7u+v‖ & \\le 7‖u‖+‖v‖ \\\\ 32 & \\le 7‖u‖+4 \\\\ 28 & \\le 7‖u‖ \\\\ 4 & \\le ‖u‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖u‖$ is $4.$" 13194139534779 true] [17592186309704 1017 #edb/ref 17592186309705 13194139534779 true] [17592186309704 1089 "q-140459" 13194139534779 true] [17592186309704 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534779 true] [17592186309704 1100 "Given that $‖5u-4v‖=26$ and $‖u‖=2$, find the smallest possible value of $‖v‖.$" 13194139534779 true] [17592186309704 1113 #uuid "65b81769-10fa-5dce-86a3-3a97e46cc0ba" 13194139534779 true] [17592186309704 1145 "Using the triangle inequality, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖5u-4v‖ & \\le ‖5u‖+‖-4v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying and substituting in the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖5u-4v‖ & \\le 5‖u‖+4‖v‖ \\\\ 26 & \\le 5\\cdot 2+4‖v‖ \\\\ 26 & \\le 10+4‖v‖ \\\\ 16 & \\le 4‖v‖ \\\\ 4 & \\le ‖v‖.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible value of $‖v‖$ is $4.$" 13194139534779 true] [17592186309705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309706 13194139534779 true] [17592186309705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309707 13194139534779 true] [17592186309705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309708 13194139534779 true] [17592186309705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309709 13194139534779 true] [17592186309705 1000 #edb/ref 17592186309710 13194139534779 true] [17592186309705 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534779 true] [17592186309705 1041 "selection" 13194139534779 true] [17592186309705 1104 #edb/ref 17592186309709 13194139534779 true] [17592186309705 1110 #uuid "a7847158-31d8-5468-ac51-329fb39a9f45" 13194139534779 true] [17592186309706 1034 "\\frac{9}{2}" 13194139534779 true] [17592186309706 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534779 true] [17592186309706 1111 #uuid "6c67f5c8-ace8-5d01-a0dd-292ecf67870e" 13194139534779 true] [17592186309707 1034 "9" 13194139534779 true] [17592186309707 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534779 true] [17592186309707 1111 #uuid "594a6210-b380-585c-bf83-0bf3c729789b" 13194139534779 true] [17592186309708 1034 "\\frac{4}{5}" 13194139534779 true] [17592186309708 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534779 true] [17592186309708 1111 #uuid "de2b02ea-174b-5122-94